# BusinessSuite ERP — Navigation UX (Breadcrumbs & Menu Search)

## Breadcrumbs (mandatory)

Every authenticated module page must show breadcrumbs so users know where they are.

- Place crumbs **above** the page title / module subnav (or as the first row of the module chrome).
- Pattern: `Home / Product / Page` (e.g. `Home / HRM / Employees`). Optional section crumb when useful.
- Current page is plain text (not a link). Parents are links.
- Nested routes (e.g. `/hrm/employees/[id]`) must extend the trail (… / Employees / Profile).
- Use the shared `Breadcrumbs` component (`components/common/breadcrumbs.tsx`) — do not invent ad-hoc crumb markup.
- When adding a new module page, wire crumbs via the module subnav or pass an explicit `trail`.

## Global menu search autocomplete (mandatory)

Every dashboard shell must keep the top-center **menu search** with autocomplete:

- Component: `GlobalMenuSearch` in the app shell header (already on ERP pages).
- Results come from `lib/menu-registry.ts` filtered by `canMenu(..., "view")`.
- Keyboard: ArrowUp/ArrowDown, Enter to open, Escape to close; ⌘K / Ctrl+K focus when available.
- New menus **must** be registry-driven so search stays complete.
- Do not remove or hide menu search from module layouts.

## Sidebar accordion (mandatory)

The left sidebar uses a **traditional accordion** (expand/collapse under each module):

1. Root lists all product modules the user can view (Dashboard, CRM, Sales, HRM, …).
2. Clicking the **chevron** expands/collapses that module’s child menus in place (dropdown under the module).
3. Clicking the **module label** navigates to the module home (`href`) and keeps the accordion UX.
4. Long menus use **nested accordions** (`group` on nav children): Core / Employment / Organization (etc.) are chevron rows. Click the section to expand/collapse its pages — same open/close pattern as the module row. Auto-expand the section that contains the current URL. Do not dump every child as a flat list under the module.
5. When the current URL belongs to a module, **auto-expand** that module and **highlight** the matching child (path + hash aware).
6. Active child: high-contrast (white on dark). Expanded/active module row uses teal or soft white fill.

Do not use drill-down “replace the whole sidebar with Back” for primary navigation. Do not split one product (e.g. HRM) into multiple top-level sidebar modules.

## Routes

Every sidebar `href` must resolve to a real `app/.../page.tsx` (or an in-page hash on an existing page such as `/reports#sales`). Prefer dedicated routes over `#` stubs for distinct menus (e.g. `/crm/contacts`, not `/crm/customers#contacts`).
